W("Extract-based: 'Finally a shirt flew into the air, and the constable found himself struggling with someone he could not see at all. Some people tried to help him, but found themselves hit by blows that seemed to come from nowhere.' (i) What is happening? (ii) What is the comic effect? (iii) What happens to Jaffers? (iv) What is the end of the lesson?", 4, "(i) Griffin is removing his last clothes and fighting the constable. (1) (ii) The unseen blows and a flying shirt make the scene a comic confusion. (1) (iii) Jaffers is knocked unconscious. (1) (iv) Griffin shakes himself free and nobody knows where to lay hands on him. (1)", "H"),
W("'Griffin was rather a lawless person.' Comment, with evidence, in about 100 words.", 5, "Griffin is brilliant but breaks every rule. When the landlord tried to eject him, he set the house on fire in revenge. Then he broke into a London store at night to dress and feed himself 'without regard to expense'. At Drury Lane he attacked a shopkeeper from behind and robbed him. At Iping he stole the clergyman's housekeeping money, deceived Mrs Hall and fought the constable. His use of his discovery is selfish, violent and unlawful. (5; content 3, expression 2)", "H"),
W("'How would you assess Griffin as a scientist?' Answer in about 100 words.", 5, "As a scientist Griffin is brilliant and persistent: he made 'experiment after experiment' to prove that the human body could become invisible and succeeded. However, he lacks a scientist's sense of responsibility and ethics. He used the discovery to harm others and never thought of how it could help people. He also did not plan for practical problems such as cold, hunger and clothing. He is therefore a gifted but irresponsible scientist. (5; content 3, expression 2)", "H"),
W("'Would you like to become invisible? What advantages and disadvantages do you foresee?' Write a paragraph of about 100 words.", 5, "I would not like it for long. An advantage may be to watch things unseen or to avoid trouble. But there are many disadvantages: like Griffin, one would be unable to wear ordinary clothes or be seen; one would be cold, hungry and lonely; nobody could recognise or trust you; and one could be tempted to misuse the power. Real friendships depend on being seen and known. (5; content 3, expression 2)", "H"),
W("There are invisible forces around us, for example magnetism. Name two others and say what the world would be like if we could see them.", 4, "Others include gravity, electric current, sound waves and radio waves (any two). If we could see them, we would see magnetic fields round magnets, electric currents in wires and radio waves in the air; the world would look full of patterns and lines, but possibly be confusing and hard to bear. (4)", "H"),
W("What makes glass or water transparent? Is it scientifically possible for a man to become invisible? Give your reasoned view in about 80 words.", 4, "Glass and water are transparent because they let visible light pass through instead of absorbing or scattering it. The human body, with different tissues, absorbs and scatters light and has a refractive index different from air, so it can be seen. Invisibility of a whole human body is not yet possible; scientists have made tiny objects hard to see, but a living person who could see yet not be seen is unlikely, since eyes need to absorb light. (4)", "H"),
W("Value-based: Griffin misused his discovery. Discuss the responsibility of scientists in the use of their inventions, with one real-life example.", 5, "Scientists must think about the effects of their work on people and nature. Griffin thought only of himself and brought fear and injury to innocent people. Today, for example, those who make powerful medicines, atomic energy or the internet must see that they are used for good: atomic knowledge can make electricity or bombs. Honesty, care and concern for society should guide science. (5; content 3, expression 2)", "H"),
W("Case study: A student in Sikar builds a small drone for a science fair and wants to use it to spy on her classmates' exam papers. Using the lesson, say what she should do and why.", 4, "She should refuse, for it would be cheating and breach others' privacy, as Griffin used invisibility only to rob and harm. She should use the drone for something useful, such as mapping the school ground or surveying crops, and show how science can help others. (4)", "H", wp=True),
W("Why does the author describe the humour of the furniture episode so fully? What does it add to the story?", 4, "The chair that charges legs first, the hat that leaps at Mrs Hall and the door that seems to slam itself are comic and keep the story light. The scene also shows the effect of a mysterious happening on superstitious villagers (witchcraft) and builds the tension that leads to Griffin's unmasking. (4)", "H"),
W("Write a diary entry (about 100 words) by Griffin on the night he spent in the London store.", 5, "Model: It is mid-winter, and I am cold as ice. Tonight I slipped into a big store before closing. When the doors were locked, I broke open boxes and found shoes, an overcoat and a wide-brimmed hat; now I am visible again! In the kitchen I found cold meat and coffee, and in the grocery sweets and wine. I shall sleep on a pile of quilts. I must wake early before the assistants arrive. How strange it is to be a man who must dress to be seen! (5; content 2, expression 2, format 1)", "H"),
W("Write a news report (about 100 words) on the strange events at the Iping inn.", 5, "Model: IPING - Villagers are in panic after a series of strange events at the village inn. A guest, a bandaged scientist, is suspected of robbing a clergyman's desk, though the clergyman saw nobody in the room. The landlord's wife reports that a hat and a chair attacked her, and the constable, Mr Jaffers, was knocked unconscious while trying to arrest the guest, who revealed that he had no head. The stranger has escaped. Police request that anyone who sees unexplained footprints should inform them. (5; headline/format 1, content 2, expression 2)", "H"),
W("Write a letter (about 100 words) from Mrs Hall to her sister describing the stranger who stayed at her inn.", 5, "Model: Dear Sister, We have had the strangest guest. He arrived in winter, wrapped in bandages, dark glasses and a false nose, and said he wanted solitude. He paid in advance, so I allowed his odd habits. But then money went missing from the clergyman's desk, and my own chair attacked me! When we asked him for explanations, he threw off his bandages and we saw no head. I nearly fainted. The constable came but could not hold him. I am still shaking. Yours, Mary. (5; content 2, expression 2, format 1)", "H"),
